==Solution 2==

Let <imath>AC = b</imath> and <imath>BC = a</imath> (and we know given that <imath>AB=12</imath>). Draw line segments <imath>YZ</imath> and <imath>WX</imath>. Now we have cyclic quadrilateral <imath>WXYZ.</imath> 

This means that opposite angles sum to <imath>180^{\circ}</imath>. Therefore, <imath>90 + m\angle YZA + 90 - m\angle WXB = 180</imath>. Simplifying carefully, we get <imath>m\angle YZA = m\angle WXB</imath>. Similarly, <imath>m\angle{ZYA}</imath> = <imath>m\angle{XWB}</imath>. 

That means <imath>\triangle ZYA \sim \triangle XWB</imath>. 

Setting up proportions, 
<imath>\dfrac{b}{12}=\dfrac{12}{a+b}.</imath>
Cross-multiplying we get:
<imath>b^2+ab=12^2</imath>

But also, by Pythagoras, 
<imath>b^2+a^2=12^2</imath>, so <imath>ab=a^2 \Rightarrow a=b</imath>

Therefore, <imath>\triangle ABC</imath> is an isosceles right triangle. <imath>AC=BC=\dfrac{12}{\sqrt{2}}=6\sqrt{2}</imath>, so the perimeter is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(C)}\ 12+12\sqrt{2}}</imath>

~BakedPotato66

~LegionOfAvatars

