label("$7$",(7/2,0),dir(270));

label("$1$",(0,9/2),dir(180));
label("$4$",(0,2),dir(180));

draw((0,5)--(8,0));

</asy>

The bases of these triangles are all <math>1</math>, and by symmetry, their heights are <math>4</math>, <math>\frac{5}{2}</math>, <math>4</math>, and <math>\frac{5}{2}</math>. Thus, their areas are <math>2</math>, <math>\frac{5}{4}</math>, <math>2</math>, and <math>\frac{5}{4}</math>, which add to the area of the shaded region, which is <math>\boxed{6\frac{1}{2}}</math>.

== Solution 2 ==

Find the area of the unshaded area by calculating the area of the triangles and rectangles outside of the shaded region. We can do this by splitting up the unshaded areas into various triangles and rectangles as shown.

<asy>

size(6cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(9pt));
draw((0,0)--(8,0)--(8,5)--(0,5)--cycle);
filldraw((7,0)--(8,0)--(8,1)--(0,4)--(0,5)--(1,5)--cycle,gray(0.8));

