<imath>\newline</imath> let <imath>\overline{BO}</imath>,<imath>\overline{CO}</imath> <imath>\cap</imath> <imath>\overline{AD}</imath> at P and Q respectively. 
<imath>\newline</imath> By interior angle theorem, <imath>m\angle{BPA} = m\angle{OBC}</imath> (For the sake of simplicity, we will only prove this on one side, since all triangles are congruent as above, the same applies for the right side)
<imath>\newline</imath> Notice that ABCO is a kite as <imath>AB = BC = 200</imath> and <imath>OA=OC = 200\sqrt{2}</imath>. Thus, <imath>OB</imath> bisects <imath>\angle{ABC}</imath>. This implies that <imath>\triangle{ABP}</imath>~<imath>\triangle{OAB}</imath>
<imath>\newline</imath> From this we obtain <imath>\frac{200}{200\sqrt{2}}=\frac{BP}{200}</imath>, from which we obtain <imath>BP = 100\sqrt{2}</imath> and by isosceles triangle theorem, <imath>BP = 200</imath>
<imath>\newline</imath> Additionally, we obtain <imath>m\angle{BPA}=m\angle{OPQ}</imath> by vertical angles, from which we obtain <imath>\triangle{OPQ}</imath>~<imath>\triangle{OBC}</imath>, which implies <imath>\frac{200\sqrt{2}-100\sqrt{2}}{200\sqrt{2}}=\frac{PQ}{200}</imath> <imath>\rightarrow</imath> <imath>PQ = 100.</imath>
<imath>\newline</imath> The requested length, <imath>AD =</imath> <imath>2*BP+PQ =</imath><imath>\boxed{\textbf{(E) } 500}</imath>.
~Stereotypicalmathnerd

==Remark (Morley's Trisector Theorem)==

This problem is related to [http://www.cut-the-knot.org/triangle/Morley/Naraniengar.shtml M. T. Naraniengar's proof] of [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Morley%27s_trisector_theorem Morley's Trisector Theorem]. This problem is taken from the figure of the Lemma of M. T. Naraniengar's proof, as shown below.

[[File:NaraniengarLemma.gif|200px]]

If four points <imath>Y'</imath>, <imath>Z</imath>, <imath>Y</imath>, <imath>Z'</imath> satisfy the conditions

<imath>\quad</imath> <imath>1.</imath> <imath>Y'Z = ZY = YZ'</imath> and

<imath>\quad</imath> <imath>2.</imath> <imath>\angle YZY'</imath> = <imath>\angle Z'YZ</imath> = <imath>180^{\circ} - 2a > 60^{\circ}</imath>

then they lie on a circle.


The Lemma is used to prove Morley's Trisector Theorem by constructing an equilateral triangle at <imath>YZ</imath> and extending <imath>AY'</imath> and <imath>AZ'</imath> as shown below.

