== Solution 14 (Vector Extension) ==

Let <imath>A = (0,0)</imath>, <imath>B = (1,0)</imath>, and <imath>C = (\frac{1}{2}, \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2})</imath>. By \href{https://artofproblemsolving.com}{\color{blue}{Vector Extension}}, where <imath>d= 3</imath> and <imath>x_C</imath> and <imath>y_C</imath> are the coordinates of the extended point:

* <imath>x_C = x_B + 3(x_B - x_A)</imath>
* <imath>y_C = y_B + 3(y_B - y_A)</imath>

Calculating our new vertices <imath>A'</imath>, <imath>B'</imath>, and <imath>C'</imath>:
* <imath>B' = (1 + 3(1 - 0), 0 + 3(0 - 0)) = (4,0)</imath>
* <imath>C' = (\frac{1}{2} + 3(\frac{1}{2} - 1), \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} + 3(\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} - 0)) = (-1, 2\sqrt{3})</imath>
* <imath>A' = (0 + 3(0 - \frac{1}{2}), 0 + 3(0 - \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2})) = (-\frac{3}{2}, -\frac{3\sqrt{3}}{2})</imath>

Because we extended every side by the exact same amount, the new large triangle <imath>\triangle A'B'C'</imath> stays perfectly equilateral. We can find its new side length squared by checking the distance between <imath>B'</imath> and <imath>C'</imath>:
<cmath> \text{Side}^2 = (-1 - 4)^2 + (2\sqrt{3} - 0)^2 = 25 + 12 = 37 </cmath>

The area of any equilateral triangle is just <imath>\frac{\sqrt{3}}{4} \times \text{side}^2</imath>. Since our starting triangle had a side length squared of <imath>1</imath> and our new one has a side length squared of <imath>37</imath>, the new area is exactly <imath>37</imath> times larger.

Hence <imath>[\triangle A'B'C']:[\triangle ABC] = \boxed{\textbf{(E) } 37:1}</imath>

~vivivviicctorrr1098

