==Solution 1 (Casework)==
For <imath>1\leq k\leq 12,</imath> suppose that cards <imath>1, 2, \ldots, k</imath> are picked up on the first pass. It follows that cards <imath>k+1,k+2,\ldots,13</imath> are picked up on the second pass.

<b>Once we pick the spots for the cards on the first pass, there is only one way to arrange all <imath>\boldsymbol{13}</imath> cards.</b> 

For each value of <imath>k,</imath> there are <imath>\binom{13}{k}-1</imath> ways to pick the <imath>k</imath> spots for the cards on the first pass: We exclude the arrangement
<asy>
size(11cm);
draw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,3)--(0,3)--cycle);
label("1", (1,1.5));
draw((3,0)--(5,0)--(5,3)--(3,3)--cycle);
label("2", (4,1.5));
draw((6,0)--(8,0)--(8,3)--(6,3)--cycle);
label("3", (7,1.5));
draw((9,0)--(11,0)--(11,3)--(9,3)--cycle);
label("4", (10,1.5));
draw((12,0)--(14,0)--(14,3)--(12,3)--cycle);
label("5", (13,1.5));
draw((15,0)--(17,0)--(17,3)--(15,3)--cycle);
label("6", (16,1.5));
draw((18,0)--(20,0)--(20,3)--(18,3)--cycle);
label("7", (19,1.5));
draw((21,0)--(23,0)--(23,3)--(21,3)--cycle);
label("8", (22,1.5));
draw((24,0)--(26,0)--(26,3)--(24,3)--cycle);
label("9", (25,1.5));
draw((27,0)--(29,0)--(29,3)--(27,3)--cycle);
label("10", (28,1.5));
draw((30,0)--(32,0)--(32,3)--(30,3)--cycle);
label("11", (31,1.5));
