==Solution 4==
Interestingly, we find that the pentagon we need is the one that is represented by the intersection of perpendicular bisectors of the connection from the center of the pentagon to one vertex. Through similar triangles and the golden ratio, we find that the side length ratio of the two pentagons is <imath>\frac{\sqrt{5}-1}{2}</imath> Thus, the answer is <imath>(\sqrt{5}+1) \cdot (\frac{\sqrt{5}-1}{2})^2 = \sqrt{5}-1</imath>. <imath>\boxed{\text{B}}</imath>
