==Solution 9==

Let the smaller semicircle <imath>\omega</imath> be concentric with the larger semicircle <imath>\Omega</imath>. Let <imath>\operatorname{rad}(\omega) = r</imath> and <imath>\operatorname{rad}(\Omega) = R</imath>. Then, by Pythagoreans, <imath>R^2 = 64+r^2</imath>. By Power of a Point, if <imath>T</imath> is the tangential point, then <imath>\operatorname{Pow}(T) \implies 64 = (R-r)r</imath>, or <imath>Rr-r^2 = 64</imath>, hence <imath>R^2 = Rr</imath>, so <imath>R(R-r) = 0</imath>. We see <imath>R \neq 0</imath>, so <imath>R = r</imath>. Then, that implies <imath>\omega = \Omega</imath> hence the chord is actually the diameter. Therefore, the area is <imath>\frac{1}{2} \pi \cdot 64 = \boxed{32\pi}</imath>.

~Pinotation

